# Template for the ISOLATED test environment used by `npm test`.
#
# Copy this to `.env.test` for local runs (already gitignored — see
# .gitignore's `.env*` rule) and fill in a LOCAL/CI-ONLY MongoDB instance.
#
# scripts/assertTestDbSafety.mjs runs automatically before every `npm test`
# (npm's `pretest` hook) and refuses to proceed if:
#   - NODE_ENV isn't "test" (the npm script sets this for you)
#   - MONGO_URI_TEST equals MONGO_URI
#   - MONGO_URI_TEST looks like a hosted/production database (Atlas, etc.)
#
# NEVER put real production values in this file. NEVER copy values from the
# real .env here. Every value below should be a throwaway, local-only, or
# CI-generated credential.

# A local (or CI service-container) MongoDB running AS A REPLICA SET.
# Order-creation tests use a real Mongoose transaction (session.withTransaction),
# which MongoDB only supports on a replica set — a plain standalone mongod
# will make those specific tests skip themselves with a clear message
# instead of failing. See .github/workflows/ci.yml for how CI provisions one.
MONGO_URI_TEST=mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/tahos_test?replicaSet=rs0&directConnection=true

# HISTORICAL NOTE (obsolete, not required): Phase 1 used JWT_SECRET /
# JWT_EXPIRES_IN here, and every test file's `canRun` gate checked
# `!!process.env.JWT_SECRET` as a proxy for "the test environment is
# configured." As of Phase 2, jsonwebtoken is uninstalled and no code path
# signs or verifies a JWT anymore — sessions are opaque, hashed, random
# tokens (see the Phase 2 section below) — and every test file's gate was
# updated to check only `dbReady` (MONGO_URI_TEST reachability), which is
# the thing that actually determines whether these suites can run. Setting
# these two vars now does nothing at all; do not set them, and do not treat
# their absence as a reason a suite would skip.

# Used by the password-reset-link tests, overridden per-test-case in code —
# this value only matters for any test that doesn't explicitly set it itself.
CLIENT_URL=http://localhost:3000

# =============================================================================
# Phase 2 — session/cookie authentication (see .env.example for full detail)
# =============================================================================
#
# Nothing below is REQUIRED for `npm run test:core` / `npm run test:http`:
# every Phase 2 test builds same-origin Request objects directly (see
# tests/helpers/testDb.mjs's requestAs()), so lib/csrf.js's no-APP_ORIGIN
# same-origin fallback already applies, and lib/session.js's own defaults
# (30-day expiry, 10 sessions/user) are fine for a test run. Uncomment only
# to deliberately exercise a non-default value in a specific test run.
#
# APP_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000
# SESSION_MAX_AGE_DAYS=30
# MAX_SESSIONS_PER_USER=10
#
# Test-cookie behavior differs between the two test commands — not a bug,
# but easy to get confused by:
#   - `npm run test:core` (tests/*.test.mjs) calls Route Handlers directly,
#     in-process, under NODE_ENV=test — lib/cookies.js issues the plain
#     "tahos_session" / "tahos_csrf" names with Secure=false.
#   - `npm run test:http` (tests/http/*.test.mjs) talks to a REAL `next
#     start` server (scripts/httpTestServer.mjs) — and `next start` always
#     runs as a production server regardless of NODE_ENV, confirmed
#     empirically for this closure. That server issues the PRODUCTION
#     cookie shape: "__Host-tahos_session", Secure, HttpOnly, the full
#     __Host- contract. tests/http/authSessionCsrfSse.integration.test.mjs
#     is written against that real shape, not the test-mode one.
# See tests/session.test.mjs for the in-process test-mode/production-mode
# cookie-attribute tests, and tests/http/authSessionCsrfSse.integration.test.mjs
# for the real-HTTP production-shape evidence.

# Cloudinary/SMTP are intentionally NOT listed here: the current Phase 1
# test suite never calls the real Cloudinary SDK or a real SMTP transport —
# uploads aren't exercised yet, and email sending is mocked via
# node:test's mock.module("nodemailer", ...) in every test that would
# otherwise trigger a real send. Add real (still test-only) values here only
# once a future phase adds tests that need them.

# =============================================================================
# Phase 3 — rate limiting / client-IP trust (see .env.example for full detail)
# =============================================================================
#
# VERCEL is never set to "1" for any test run — neither `npm run
# test:core`'s direct-handler suite nor the tests/http harness's real
# `next start` server. This means every test, by default, exercises the
# GENERIC reverse-proxy tier (tier 2), never the Vercel tier (tier 1) —
# which is correct, since none of these processes actually run on Vercel's
# infrastructure. The Vercel-path SELECTION/VALIDATION logic itself (in
# lib/clientIp.js) is instead tested directly in tests/rateLimit.test.mjs
# using its test-only `ipAddressFn` dependency-injection option, and via a
# module-level mock of the "@vercel/functions" package for tests that need
# to exercise a real route end-to-end without that injection point —
# never by actually setting VERCEL=1 against real Vercel infrastructure,
# which no local or CI test environment has access to.
#
# TRUST_PROXY_HEADERS (tier 2) is left unset in this file: tests that
# specifically need to exercise the generic-proxy code path set
# process.env.TRUST_PROXY_HEADERS / TRUSTED_PROXY_HOP_COUNT directly,
# in-process, for the duration of that one test only, and restore them
# afterward.
#
# The tests/http harness's real `next start` server IS configured with
# TRUST_PROXY_HEADERS=true / TRUSTED_PROXY_HOP_COUNT=1 — but that's set
# directly in scripts/httpTestServer.mjs's spawn() call (see its own
# comment), not here, because the port (and therefore nothing about the
# identity model) isn't known until the harness picks one at runtime. This
# is deliberate, not a weaker default: `next start` always runs as
# production regardless of NODE_ENV, and register/reset-password's ONLY
# dimension is client IP — without this, the harness's own server would
# 503 on every register/reset-password call by default (correctly
# reproducing what an unconfigured, non-Vercel production deployment would
# do), which would leave no way to exercise the success path against a
# real server at all. The fail-closed "no trust configured, unrecognized
# platform" path itself is instead proven directly in
# tests/rateLimit.test.mjs by forcing NODE_ENV=production in-process — the
# exact same code path a real unconfigured deployment hits, without
# needing a second real server.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_*_MAX values are also left unset here deliberately: every
# rate-limit test either drives the real, code-reviewed defaults (see
# lib/rateLimitConfig.js) directly, or calls lib/rateLimit.js's
# checkRateLimit()/resolveBoundedInt() with an explicit limit inline —
# nothing in the suite depends on an env override.
